<p>Token and context figures for the fixed-<code>xhigh</code> run were not recorded in the
published bench log.</p>
<p>The judge made 825 decisions in the Auto run: 591 <code>medium</code>, 127 <code>high</code>, 105
<code>low</code>, 2 <code>xhigh</code>. This is a single pass, so one or two tasks either way is
within run-to-run variance; the fixed-<code>xhigh</code> run also had web search off. Raw
records and method: <a href="../benchmarks/terminal-bench-2.1/README.md#bench-log">Bench log</a>.</p>
<h2>How a decision is made</h2>
<ol>
<li>A small local model, the <strong>effort judge</strong>, reads the request (or the step:
the task request, the agent&#39;s text for this round, and up to three tool
calls with their results). It returns probabilities for four levels:
easy, normal, hard and very hard.</li>
<li>Auto starts from <code>medium</code> on every turn and moves along the model&#39;s
supported efforts: easy → <code>low</code>, normal → <code>medium</code>, hard → <code>high</code>, very
hard → <code>xhigh</code>. A run of hard turns never ratchets the effort upward, and
auto never chooses <code>none</code>, <code>minimal</code>, <code>max</code> or <code>ultra</code>. If you pick <code>max</code> or
<code>ultra</code> yourself, that choice is left alone.</li>
<li>The level is chosen by minimising expected cost under the judge&#39;s
probabilities, not by taking the most likely level. Calling a hard request
easy costs more than the reverse, because under-thinking degrades the
answer while over-thinking only costs tokens.</li>
</ol>
<p>After each tool batch the step judgment is stored on that batch&#39;s last tool
result, so later requests rebuild the same history and the prompt cache keeps
hitting.</p>
<h2>When it applies</h2>
<p>Auto changes effort only where doing so does not invalidate the provider&#39;s
prompt cache:</p>
<ul>
<li>Anthropic models that accept a mid-conversation effort change: Claude Opus
5+, Sonnet 5.5+, Haiku 5.5+, Fable 5.1+ and Mythos 5.1+.</li>
<li>OpenAI GPT-6 and later through the Responses API.</li>
<li>Custom Chat/Responses routes whose model declares effort levels (the effort
is sent with each request).</li>
</ul>
<p>It judges user-written prompts and agent briefs, not runtime-injected turns. A
model that turns out to reject the change is switched off for auto at runtime.</p>
<p>If the judge is missing, still loading, slow, or fails, the turn simply uses
the default effort. A judgment normally has 0.4 s. The model starts loading in
the background when a session is created or opened (about 2.3 s on a desktop
CPU), so the first turn normally finds it ready; if it does not, that turn
waits up to about 3 s for the load.</p>
<h2>The judge model</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>Base</td>
<td>EmbeddingGemma 2 text encoder, 18 of 24 layers, with a four-level classification head</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Format</td>
<td>ONNX, 4-bit weights, about 150 MB</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Runtime</td>
<td>ONNX Runtime on the CPU (4 threads); no GPU, no network after download</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Input</td>
<td>The cleaned request (up to 1,500 characters) and the end of the previous reply (and, in some formats, the previous request), or the step text, all packed into one shared token budget (256 by default, set by the model&#39;s calibration; the layout is format-dependent, and the pack format caps the request tokens separately). Anything beyond the budget is cut from the end</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Speed</td>
<td>About 115 ms per judgment (median), under 200 ms at the slowest, on a desktop CPU; the first load takes about 2.5 s</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>The model is downloaded once from the Mixdog GitHub release named in
<a href="../src/runtime/effort-judge/model-manifest.json"><code>model-manifest.json</code></a> and
checked against its sha256 values. A newer manifest replaces the installed
files in place; a partially updated folder is never loaded. Nothing you type
is sent anywhere for this judgment.</p>
<h2>Accuracy</h2>
<p>Compared with Jev 1.13 (TypeSafe&#39;s general decision API, given the same four
levels and criteria) on held-out items, none used in training:
coding-agent prompts from held-out users, coding-agent sessions, SWE-bench
issues, agent task requests, two chat sets, a final held-out set, and agent
tool-result steps. The reference is three GPT-6.1 Sol votes per item (low
effort), judged on the same text the judge sees; the level is the rounded
mean of the known votes, and items where all three votes were undecidable
are left out. That leaves 12,067 labelled items, of which 11,649 also have a
Jev answer; the tables use those 11,649 (418 items have no Jev answer). On
all 12,067 items, v7 agrees exactly on 77.9% and v6 on 71.0%.
Differences are paired on the same items; intervals are 95% bootstrap
intervals (2,000 resamples), and McNemar&#39;s test compares items only one of
the two judged exactly right.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>All items (n = 11,649)</th>
<th align="right">Judge v7</th>
<th align="right">Judge v6</th>
<th align="right">Jev 1.13</th>
<th align="right">v7 − Jev [95% CI]</th>
</tr>
</thead>
