<p>Judge v7 is also ahead of the previous judge, v6: +7.0 pts exact agreement
[6.2, 7.8] on the same items.</p>
<p>How to read it:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Ceiling.</strong> The reference itself is not perfectly repeatable: one GPT
vote matches the rounded mean of the other two on 91.2% of items with three
known votes (weighted kappa 0.907). That is the practical upper bound for
exact agreement, so most of the remaining differences are one-level calls
on borderline items, not mistakes.</li>
<li><strong>Where v7 is better.</strong> Higher agreement and kappa, smaller average error,
and better hard-work detection, all with intervals that exclude zero; the
exact-level advantage is significant on requests and on agent steps.</li>
<li><strong>Where they are even.</strong> Large errors on agent steps are equally common
(6.4% for both), and agent steps are the weakest case for every judge. The
share of two-or-more-level errors is also the same for v7 and v6 overall
(2.5%).</li>
<li>Jev is a general model and was not tuned for this task; the comparison
shows what a small dedicated model gains on its one job, not a general
ranking of the two.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Effect on cost</h2>
<p>Measured with an earlier judge (v1); a re-run with v7 is pending.
Against a fixed <code>high</code> effort on the same tasks, with every run passing:
GPT-6.1 Sol produced 46% fewer output tokens on five hard tasks and 26% fewer
on eight Terminal-Bench tasks. Claude Opus 5.5 was about unchanged (±3%),
since it sizes its own thinking regardless of the effort setting.</p>
<h2>Limitations</h2>
<ul>
<li>The judge sees only a limited, shared token budget (256 by default) holding
the start of the request and the end of the previous reply,
so work implied by earlier conversation can be under-rated.</li>
<li>On 43% of the requests the reference rates hard or very hard, Auto picks a
lower effort, mostly one level lower (82% of those). <code>xhigh</code> is picked on
1.5% of items against 3.3% in the reference, so the hardest tasks can still
run at <code>high</code>.</li>
<li>Reference levels are LLM judgments, not measured task outcomes.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Turning it off</h2>
<p>Auto reasoning effort is a built-in under <strong>Extensions → Built-in</strong>. For
diagnostics, <code>MIXDOG_AUTO_EFFORT=off|observe|on</code> overrides it (<code>observe</code>
judges and logs without applying), and <code>MIXDOG_AUTO_EFFORT_STEPS=off</code> keeps
auto to the first request of each turn.</p>
<h2>Training data</h2>
<p>The judge is fine-tuned from EmbeddingGemma 2 (Apache License 2.0). Its
training data includes requests and agent steps from SWE-chat (ODC-BY 1.0),
OpenAssistant oasst2 (Apache 2.0), WildChat (ODC-BY 1.0), DataClaw exports
(MIT), DevGPT (CC BY 4.0), issue texts from the SWE-bench training split
(MIT), and agent steps from NVIDIA&#39;s SWE-Hero OpenHands trajectories
(CC BY 4.0). Judge v7 adds Stack Exchange posts (CC BY-SA 2.5/3.0/4.0, via
HuggingFaceTB/stackexchange_2025_md); GitHub issues and pull requests of
permissively licensed repositories (via common-pile/github_archive); Nebius
SWE-rebench-V2 and SWE-rebench-V2-PRs (CC BY 4.0); and agent trajectories
from nebius/SWE-agent-trajectories and
nebius/SWE-rebench-openhands-trajectories (CC BY 4.0), Kwai-Klear SWE-smith
mini-swe-agent-plus, SWE-Factory DeepSWE-Agent Kimi-K2, SWE-Gym
OpenHands-SFT and TIGER-Lab SWE-Next-SFT (MIT), and AlienKevin SWE-ZERO-12M
and Lego-X SWE-Lego real and synthetic (Apache 2.0). The release notes of
each <code>effort-judge-v*</code> release list the files, hashes and attributions.</p>
<p>Implementation: <a href="../src/runtime/agent/orchestrator/providers/auto-effort.mjs">decision rule</a>,
<a href="../src/runtime/agent/orchestrator/session/manager/ask-turn-auto-effort.mjs">turn judgment</a>,
<a href="../src/runtime/agent/orchestrator/session/loop/step-auto-effort.mjs">step judgment</a>,
<a href="../src/runtime/effort-judge/judge-worker.mjs">judge worker</a>.</p>
